    8 │ 	.index("by_author_and_channel", { fields: ["author", "channel"] } satisfies object)!
    9 │ 	.index("by_author_and_channel_and_body", ["author", "channel", "body"]);
  
  i The index by_author_and_channel is defined here.
  
     6 │ (defineTable({ author: v.string(), channel: v.string() }) as any)
     7 │ 	.index("by_author" as const, ["author"] as const)
   > 8 │ 	.index("by_author_and_channel", { fields: ["author", "channel"] } satisfies object)!
       │ 	 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
     9 │ 	.index("by_author_and_channel_and_body", ["author", "channel", "body"]);
    10 │ 
  
  i Every index slows down inserts and updates and takes up storage. Queries that use by_author find the same documents with by_author_and_channel, so delete by_author and use by_author_and_channel instead.
  
  i Keep both indexes only if you need the documents in a different order: by_author sorts matching documents by _creationTime, while by_author_and_channel sorts them by channel.
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

